<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bertrand Russell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bertrand Arthur William Russell, 3rd Earl Russell</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bertrand Russell, 3rd Earl Russell, was an English mathematician and philosopher and a founder of analytic philosophy, who did pioneering work in logic, set theory and the philosophy of language. A grandson of the prime minister John Russell, 1st Earl Russell, he studied mathematics at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and with Alfred North Whitehead wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> Mathematica (1910–1913), a major attempt to reduce the whole of mathematics to logic. He went to prison for his pacifism during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, later condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>' involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and campaigned for nuclear disarmament. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1950.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell.jpg" alt="Bertrand Russell" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bertrand_Russell_in_1936.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bertrand Russell</b> held that tariffs, sanitary regulation and a just system of distribution could hardly be managed without central government, and Robert Nozick argued in Anarchy, State, and Utopia that a minimal “night-watchman state” would arise from anarchy by an invisible hand.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bertrand Russell</b> argued that it weakens the individual’s capacity to judge his country’s foreign policy; Albert Einstein called it “an infantile disease … the measles of mankind”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
